    "turn_idx": 3,
    "turn_text": "Well, not the electric bill has gone a little bit dark, although a couple of the players in the action have tried to change the facts on the ground in a way that increases their cloud by Exelon, for example, saying it is going to go ahead and move forward with its plans to shut down a couple of its nuclear plans. So that's politics and really effective in its way because it shows the downside of not getting to a deal. On the other issue, the elected school board, Delia Ramirez, who's the house sponsor of the bill, has agreed we are told by capital facts to meet with Mayor Lightfoot as soon as tomorrow. to talk about this. They're determined that Ramirez and others in the House are determined to stick with the 21 person board, which has been like for its chief complaint. Everybody is scratching their heads over the mayor's threat to withhold Chicago funding from the schools if the school board is independent of the mayor. And that would include not only operational funds, but possibly even the city's obligations to catch up with the drastic pension underfunding in the Chicago schools. That would be a real hot-button issue and probably would seriously damage the mayor's electoral prospects. But nevertheless, she's dangling that, apparently, as a bargaining chip. And we'll see. The school board thing will play out, we think, over the next few days, the electric bill may take a little bit longer.",
