  {
    "episode_id": 71227,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/1/71227.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 5,
    "turn_text": "Well, I guess it's a new day in the sense that Welch did go ahead and put in term limits on legislative leaders, for example, he did. There was no evidence that he refused to move bills out of committee in the way, out of the Rules Committee and the way that Speaker Mike Matigan used to. There was an effort in places to actually work with the Republicans, but none of that was, that latter point was very serious. This is a totally democratic dominated legislature. And with just about every bill that passed was passed in the company of Republican leadership, speaking out about the atrocities contained therein. It's a little bit like what we're seeing on the national scene where President Biden is talking a lot about a bipartisanship, but not really getting any bipartisanship in Illinois. The Democrats are talking bipartisanship, but they don't need Republican votes, and they're not making much of an effort to get Republican votes.",
