    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/1/71227.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 6,
    "turn_text": "We certainly have heard from Democratic lawmakers who say they feel like they have more of a voice now. It's not so much one man ruled. The speaker is listening to them. But undoubtedly, Republicans still feel that they have the status that being in the super minority of the legislature will always earn you, which is no status whatsoever. And that does not seem to have changed. It's full disclosure with David Greising, president and CEO of the Better Government Association. We've got a couple of other legislative things we want to delve into, but I want to steer you, David, a little bit to the topic of journalism. I know this is something the BGA is very involved in. You do a lot of work with various media outlets. You do awards for investigative reporting. You understand innately the crucial importance of journalism at the local and at the state level in keeping government honest, and we've all been watching this week the fallout from the takeover of the Chicago Tribune and the Tribune Company by a hedge fund group, Alden Capital, and what we're now seeing as a pretty sizable of editorial staff from the Tribune, including most of their leading columnists and some other reporters and editors and things. A lot of people are wondering if this is the start of the death knell for the Tribune. And while reports of its demise may be premature, it does have people wondering again what this means for the future of journalism here in Illinois.",
