  },
  {
    "episode_id": 71227,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/1/71227.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 7,
    "turn_text": "Well, just focusing narrowly on the Tribune for now, the Tribune, as we've known it, has passed really into history in many respects. The loss of those high-profile colonists is emblematic of what's happening through the ranks of the newspaper. Other top-notch journalists have left the Tribune either before the buyouts or as part of the buyouts. And many who haven't already left are on the market, as I can tell you, as a news organization that receives resumes and outreach from various really top, not very, very good journalists. We recruited David Jackson, probably the best investigative reporter in the state about six months ago, in part because of all the uncertainty going on at the Tribune, and others are leading to go and pursue other ventures. The real threat to journalism would be if everybody that were talking about leads journalism altogether, Some of the columnists who have left have said, oh, I'm going to set up a blog or a podcast or a newsletter or something. We'll see how that all plays out. In Springfield coverage, the nonprofit journalism is helping out a little bit. The Capitol News Illinois Bureau really has done some very good work. It doesn't altogether make up for the huge loss in the coverage from Springfield of years past, but it's a big improvement over what we had seen over the last few years. The BGA is a nonprofit and we're stepping in with high rings like I just mentioned and along with many other nonprofit news organizations, we're sort of filling in some of the gaps that have been created. And I think there's a move a foot to for some of the philanthropic foundations to to double down on the investments they've made in nonprofit journalism. And so nonprofit journalism today is more connected to local communities than the historic, the big Tribune, and sometimes really ever worse. Some of those are based in local communities. So that's good. As regards to downstate news, the bloodletting there, Jim, is in many ways worse than what we've seen at the Tribune because the staffs were smaller to begin with. They had less room for error. And there have been significant cutbacks in your own newspaper in Springfield, significant cutbacks. And really, we look to statewide news organizations as prospective partners on investigations. We just don't really have anybody to work with it. A lot of the newspapers anymore because their ranks are so thin. And so that is going to really hurt accountability coverage in particular. But even just basic news coverage is really, there's a crisis going on. not just in the Chicago journalism but in statewide news coverage as well.",
