    "turn_text": "And a battle cry to go out to say support local journalism where you can. I know it's frustrating people got conditioned over the last 20 years or so to have a lot of things just available for free on the internet. But you know good quality journalism does require support and so if you can subscribe to a newspaper some sort of legit journalism source you're doing not only good for yourself to keep yourself edified but good for your community because again journalism is what shines that light when government tries to to go too far We are back on full disclosure. David Greising, President and CEO of the Better Government Association is here with us as we go through some of what government is doing that you need to be aware of. And part of this has to do with just the process, the means by which government carries out its tasks. Bit of an uproar last week, David, when the Illinois House reconvened. One of the big things they had to do was to pass a fix to change the fact that when the budget was initially approved, it didn't have proper effective dates for various provisions, which meant that most of the budget, the money couldn't have been spent until next June, 11 months into the fiscal year, a serious problem. The governor needed to fix through an inventory veto, but the House had to sign off on it. They didn't have the votes necessarily present to do that though, so they hurriedly passed a rule change to allow on that day for remote voting so that several lawmakers could weigh in and cast those votes directly. Republicans are very upset about this, but David, it does beg the question, particularly because the legislators were visible on camera. They had to state their votes So it's not as though somebody was just randomly logging on and pretending to be a lawmaker and casting their vote. We still know what they did and how they did it. Is it really that big a deal to allow remote voting? Or is this something we should be thinking about as a wave of the future for the 21st century?",
