    "turn_idx": 17,
    "turn_text": "Exactly. I think it's a good change. The sexist definition of that position in government is the sell by day that has passed a long time ago. It's interesting that the state did this. It would have been, if there were a better relationship between the mayor of the city of Chicago and the governor of the state of Illinois, that might have been something done jointly, or at least a joint press conference that shows you a little bit about the continuing sort of uneasy relationship between those two. But I do think that that's a long overdue change in the nomenclature there. As a journalist, whenever I have to use that term, it really bothers me, frankly, to be honest with you. as regards some of the other changes that you're talking about. Yeah, these are some important changes, moving a date of voting is a big deal. That can affect who's eligible, what the politics are, are different in any given time, et cetera. So that's important. And then the expansion of voting, it's nice to hear about somebody somewhere expanding voting. I know that this is controversial, but in this day of voter suppression nationwide, whatever you might think of the particulars in this specific case, it's nice that at least in one state people were trying to talk about it, trying to get more people of the ballot instead of limiting access to the ballot.",
